     The ppffiill framework allows for a specified function or a list of functions
     to be invoked for every incoming or outgoing packet for a particular net‐
     work I/O stream.  These hooks may be used to implement a firewall or per‐
     form packet transformations.

     Packet filtering points, for historical reasons named _h_e_a_d_s, are regis‐
     tered with ppffiill__hheeaadd__rreeggiisstteerr().  The function is supplied with special
     versioned _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_f_i_l___h_e_a_d___a_r_g_s structure that specifies type and fea‐
     tures of the head as well as human readable name.  If the filtering point
     to be ever destroyed, the subsystem that created it must unregister it
     with call to ppffiill__hheeaadd__uunnrreeggiisstteerr().

     Packet filtering systems may register arbitrary number of filters, for
     historical reasons named _h_o_o_k_s.  To register a new hook ppffiill__aadddd__hhooookk()
     with special versioned _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_f_i_l___h_o_o_k___a_r_g_s structure is called.  The
     structure specifies type and features of the hook, pointer to the actual
     filtering function and user readable name of the filtering module and
     ruleset name.  Later hooks can be removed with ppffiill__rreemmoovvee__hhooookk() func‐
     tions.

     To connect existing _h_o_o_k to an existing _h_e_a_d function ppffiill__lliinnkk() shall
     be used.  The function is supplied with versioned _s_t_r_u_c_t _p_f_i_l___l_i_n_k___a_r_g_s
     structure that specifies either literal names of hook and head or point‐
     ers to them.  Typically ppffiill__lliinnkk() is called by filtering modules to au‐
     toregister their default ruleset and default filtering points.  It also
     serves on the kernel side of ioctl(2) when user changes ppffiill configura‐
     tion with help of pfilctl(8) utility.

     For every packet traveling through a _h_e_a_d the latter shall invoke
     ppffiill__rruunn__hhooookkss().  The function can accept either _s_t_r_u_c_t _m_b_u_f _* pointer
     or a _v_o_i_d _* pointer and length.  In case if a hooked filtering module
     cannot understand _v_o_i_d _* pointer ppffiill will provide it with a fake one.
     All calls to ppffiill__rruunn__hhooookkss() are performed in network epoch(9).

HHEEAADDSS ((ffiilltteerriinngg ppooiinnttss))
     By default kernel creates the following heads:

     inet      IPv4 packets.

     inet6     IPv6 packets.

     ethernet  Link-layer packets.

     Default rulesets are automatically linked to these heads to preserve his‐
     torical behaviour.

